---
Source control for coding-agent swarms

Usage: so [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Start here:
  init        Create a store in a directory
  clone       Make a store from a git remote
  info        Describe the store
  whoami      Show the identity your changes carry
  update      Replace this program with the latest release

Record and land changes:
  ws          Workspaces: edit in a directory of your own, commit, land
  commit      Record a changeset from a list of changes
  land        Merge a change onto a ref and publish it there
  land-check  Preview a land: clean or conflict, writing nothing
  land-group  Land several changes in order as one operation
  rebase      Merge a change onto a ref's head, moving no ref
  revert      Take back a changeset or a range by landing its inverse
  blob        Store content as a blob for a commit to name

Checks:
  check       List the checks a tree describes

Read history:
  log         List history along first parents, newest first
  show        Show one changeset
  diff        List the files that differ between two revisions
  cat         Print one file at a revision, or part of it
  ls          List one directory at a revision
  history     List the changesets that changed a path
  ancestry    Compare two revisions' ancestry
  merge-base  Find where a revision forks from another
  resolve     Print the changeset id a revision names

Refs and operations:
  ref         List refs, or move one by compare-and-swap
  op          List the store's operations, or undo one

Git and remotes:
  git         Bring history in from git, or send it back
  remote      Add, remove or list the store's git remotes
  fetch       Fetch a git remote into the store
  push        Send a ref to a git remote's branch, never by force
  sync        Land main's unpublished changes on a remote, and push
  auth        Keep, list or remove your logins, or hand one to git

Store upkeep:
  verify      Check the store's consistency
  gc          Remove the store's leftovers, never an object
  repair      Rebuild what the journal can rebuild, then verify
  upgrade     Move the store to the latest format (irreversible)

Agents and servers:
  mcp         Serve the store's operations to an agent over MCP
  serve       Serve stores to stock git over ssh or HTTP
  derive      Fill the read cache for new changesets ahead of readers
  schema      Print the JSON Schema of the request and response types

Examples:
  $ export SWARMOPS_REPO="$PWD/acme" SWARMOPS_AGENT=person/ada
  $ so init acme
  $ echo '[]' | so commit -m root --changes - --allow-empty --advance main
  $ so ws create fix-1
  $ echo hello > fix-1/README.md
  $ so ws commit --ws fix-1 -m "Add a readme"
  $ so ws land --ws fix-1
  $ so --human log

Output:
  Every command prints one JSON envelope on stdout, or a short text with --human
  or SWARMOPS_FORMAT=human, and exits 0 or with its error's code. mcp, serve,
  derive --follow and auth git-credential speak their own protocols there. The
  program alone, or a command group alone (ws, auth, ...), is a usage error,
  exit 2: at a terminal it prints its help instead of the envelope, unless
  --json or SWARMOPS_FORMAT=json is given.

Learn more:
  A command's help      so <command> --help
  Quick start           https://swarmops.cc/docs/guides/quick-start
  Every command         https://swarmops.cc/docs/reference/cli
  Envelope, exit codes  https://swarmops.cc/docs/develop/cli-scripting
  Error codes           https://swarmops.cc/docs/reference/errors

Options:
      --repo <STORE>     The store to use (default: $SWARMOPS_REPO)
      --as <AGENT>       The agent to act as, <family>/<instance> (default:
                         $SWARMOPS_AGENT)
      --json             Print the JSON envelope: the default, unless
                         $SWARMOPS_FORMAT is human
      --human            Print a short text rendering instead of JSON
      --pretty[=<BOOL>]  Pretty-print the JSON (default: when stdout is a
                         terminal)
  -h, --help             Print help
  -V, --version          Print version
